<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip the Handsome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1478">1478</a>–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Philip I of Castile · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip the Handsome was the son of Emperor Maximilian I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> and inherited the Burgundian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as a child in 1482, with his father as regent. His marriage to Joanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> made him king of Castile in 1506, but he died that year. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> united the Burgundian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, Castilian and Aragonese inheritances.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome.jpg" alt="Philip the Handsome" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Legend of the Magdalene · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ma%C3%AEtre_de_la_l%C3%A9gende_de_Sainte_Marie-Madeleine_-_Philippe_le_Beau,_INV_2085_;_C_324_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> in 1478, the son of Maximilian I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> spread the rumour that the child was a girl, and at the baptism his step-grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-york">Margaret of York</a> showed the boy naked to the crowd to end the doubt. Mary died after a horse accident in March 1482, when he was four. In December 1482 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1482">Treaty of Arras</a> with Louis XI secured his rule over much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> but left the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in French hands.</p><p>In 1493 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> extended his rule to Artois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Charolais, and Maximilian, burdened as new ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, transferred power to the fifteen-year-old Philip. The Burgundian lands welcomed a native-born ruler who spoke their language and was peace-loving. At his inauguration in 1494 he abolished the Great Privilege and restored centralising policies of his great-grandfather and grandfather while abandoning their expansionism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 20 October 1496 Philip married Joanna, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a>, at Lier. His sister Margaret married Joanna's brother John, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. John died in 1497 and their sister Isabella in 1498, and after the death in 1500 of Isabella's infant son Miguel, Joanna became heir to the Castilian and Aragonese crowns. When Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> died in 1504 she left Castile to Joanna. The nobles forced Ferdinand to withdraw from the regency, and Philip was recognised as king.</p><p>In January 1506 a storm drove Philip and Joanna ashore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, and Henry VII kept them as guests for six weeks until Philip signed a treaty known as the Malus Intercursus. They landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">Corunna</a> on 28 April 1506. In meetings in June, mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a>, Ferdinand accepted that Philip and Joanna should take over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Philip was an important patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-bosch">Hieronymus Bosch</a>, whom he commissioned in 1504 to paint a large triptych of the Last Judgement, and his chapel included Pierre de La Rue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip died suddenly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> on 25 September 1506, apparently of typhoid fever, though poisoning was widely spoken of and Joanna believed it. He is entombed in the Royal Chapel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Maximilian appointed Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the States General accepted her in April 1507. His son Charles eventually united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, Burgundian, Castilian and Aragonese inheritances.</p><p>The Belgian historian Jean-Marie Cauchies writes that Philip died too young to show himself a great man and remained above all the heir of the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, personifying a prince of peace and concord. Contemporaries remarked on his good looks, which gave him his epithet, and the Venetian ambassador Vincenzo Querini called him physically beautiful.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip the Handsome” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their daughter Joanna married <b>Philip the Handsome</b>, son of Emperor Maximilian I, but Philip died in 1506 and Joanna was judged unfit to rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mary of Burgundy's death in 1482 left the Burgundian State's northern lands to her infant son <b>Philip the Handsome</b>, with her widower Maximilian of Austria as regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.22&amp;year=1496" aria-label="Bruges on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4623 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philip the Handsome"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5073 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5073 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.22&amp;year=1496">What was here in 1496? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
